<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Opechancanough</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1646">1646</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Opechancanough was paramount chief of the Powhatan Confederacy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> and a younger brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wahunsenacah">Wahunsenacah</a>. He led the surprise attacks of 1622 and 1644 that killed hundreds of English colonists in an attempt to drive them out. Captured in 1646 at a great age, he was shot by a guard at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown">Jamestown</a>, and the confederacy then became tributary to Virginia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/opechancanough.jpg" alt="Opechancanough" width="320" height="411" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Robert Vaughan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Smith_taking_the_King_of_Pamavnkee_prisoner_-_etching.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>His name is understood to mean He whose Soul is White in the Powhatan language. A younger brother, or possibly half-brother, of Chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wahunsenacah">Wahunsonacock</a>, known as Powhatan, he was sachem of a tribe on the Pamunkey River near present-day West Point when the English founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown">Jamestown</a> in May 1607. Strongly opposed to the settlers, he captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-smith-explorer">Captain John Smith</a> on the Chickahominy River and brought him before Powhatan at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/werowocomoco">Werowocomoco</a>. Some historians, among them Carl Bridenbaugh, have speculated that he was Don Luis, a chief's son taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in the 16th century whose people killed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> of the Ajacán Mission in 1571; others think him a relative of Don Luis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The peace that followed the marriage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pocahontas">Pocahontas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-rolfe">John Rolfe</a> in April 1614 ended after her death and that of Powhatan in 1618. After a brief rule by his elder brother Opitchapam, under whom he was war chief, Opechancanough became the Mamanatowick of the confederacy. As tobacco became the colony's cash crop and settlers spread plantations along the rivers, he gave up diplomacy. On 22 March 1622 coordinated attacks along both shores of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-river">James River</a> killed about a third of the settlers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>. The colony recovered, and the colonists killed hundreds of Native people in reprisal, among them warriors poisoned by Dr. John Pott at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown">Jamestown</a>. He launched a last effort to expel the colonists on 18 April 1644.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1646 forces under Royal Governor William Berkeley captured him, then thought to be between 90 and 100 years old, and paraded him through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown">Jamestown</a> before a jeering crowd. A settler guarding him in prison shot him in the back. He is reported to have said that had he taken Berkeley prisoner, he would not have exposed him as a show to his people. He was succeeded as weroance by Nectowance, then Totopotomoi, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cockacoeske">Cockacoeske</a>, believed to be his daughter or granddaughter. Contemporary reports of weakness and drooping eyelids have led to speculation that he suffered from myasthenia gravis.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Opechancanough” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Some historians have identified Don Luis with Wahunsenacah’s kinsman <b>Opechancanough</b>, but the identification is unproven.</blockquote><a href="/empire/powhatan-confederacy">Powhatan Confederacy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Warriors under <b>Opechancanough</b> struck English settlements along the James River, killing about a quarter of the colony's population.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-22">22 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/werowocomoco">Werowocomoco</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown">Jamestown</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.41,-76.65&amp;year=1636" aria-label="Werowocomoco on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Opechancanough"><image href="/maps/globes/g270n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5899 4315h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5899 4315h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.41,-76.65&amp;year=1636">What was here in 1636? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
